=== REASONING ===
Goal: produce a frozen test contract for Prompt 12 to implement against. No source edits in this
phase; only this log file changes in the working tree.

Sink injection model (echoes Prompt 10 wiring):
  - Reuse the existing internal/api.APICallLogger interface verbatim as the sink port. Rationale:
    * It is already the production async writer (asyncAPICallLogger from Prompt 10) with non-
      blocking Enqueue(*models.APICallLog), drain-on-Shutdown, and bounded queue.
    * It already persists to api_call_logs through APICallLogBatchInserter.CreateBatch.
    * Using the same interface guarantees inbound and outbound rows land in the same table with
      the same lifecycle, the same drop-on-full semantics, and the same Shutdown deadline.
  - To avoid a forbidden internal/platform/httputil -> internal/api -> internal/database edge,
    Prompt 12 will define a *local* alias interface in httputil:

        // APICallSink is the outbound persistence port. Implementations MUST be
        // non-blocking; httputil never blocks the round-trip on sink calls.
        type APICallSink interface {
            Enqueue(*models.APICallLog)         // models is internal/models (leaf, allowed)
            CaptureBodies() bool                // opt-in body capture toggle
        }

    The api.APICallLogger from Prompt 10 satisfies APICallSink structurally (Enqueue is already
    present). CaptureBodies() will be added to the production async logger so a single instance
    can serve both inbound middleware (which receives the bool as a constructor argument today)
    and the outbound sink (which reads it dynamically per round-trip). Prompt 12 may instead
    wrap the existing api.APICallLogger in a tiny adapter struct in cmd/teslasync/main.go that
    closes over cfg.APILogs.CaptureBodies; either approach satisfies the contract below as long
    as the layering rule is preserved. internal/models is a leaf package and is already imported
    by internal/api and internal/database, so importing it from internal/platform/httputil
    introduces no new cycle.

  - ClientConfig gains one optional field: Sink APICallSink. Nil means "no sink" — LoggedTransport
    behaves exactly as it does today (zerolog only). A non-nil Sink opts the call site into
    persistence to api_call_logs with the same redaction and body-capture rules as the inbound
    middleware.

  - LoggedTransport.Name continues to flow through as the api_call_logs.service tag. This is the
    existing public API of LoggedTransport — Prompt 12 must not rename or repurpose it.
    ClientConfig.Name -> LoggedTransport.Name -> APICallLog.Service.

Layering enforcement:
  - internal/platform/httputil must not import internal/database. The Sink interface is defined
    locally in httputil; the production binding lives in cmd/teslasync/main.go where database is
    already imported; the api package (which owns the async writer) is already free of an
    httputil dependency, so no cycle is introduced.
  - The static check `! grep -RE "internal/(database|api)" internal/platform/httputil/` (excluding
    test files importing internal/api/api_call_log_middleware solely for the *interface*; the
    preferred form is to import only internal/models) is a hard test assertion in the matrix
    below.

Preserving today's zerolog emission:
  - LoggedTransport's zerolog.Debug/Error log lines remain unchanged in shape. The sink call is
    additive and happens after the existing log lines are emitted, so log-scrapers and existing
    tests TestLoggedTransport_Success / _Error / _SanitizesAPIKey continue to pass without
    modification. Prompt 12 must not delete or restructure the existing zerolog log calls.

Non-blocking guarantee:
  - The sink call is a single Enqueue(*models.APICallLog) — by the Prompt 10 contract Enqueue
    MUST NOT block. The test matrix asserts this with a deliberately slow sink (sleep 250ms in
    Enqueue) and verifies that round-trip latency observed by the caller is bounded by
    transport latency + a small delta (<5ms) and is never increased by sink latency.

Body capture model:
  - Sink.CaptureBodies() returns the live toggle (cfg.APILogs.CaptureBodies). When false, the
    persisted row has nil request_body / response_body. When true, both are captured and each
    is independently truncated to 10 KB with the existing inbound truncation marker
    (matches Prompt 10 cappedBuffer).
  - Capture must use a TeeReader on the request body and a wrapped resp.Body so the downstream
    consumer still receives the original bytes. The test matrix asserts byte-for-byte equality
    of the response body delivered to the caller before and after enabling capture.

Network error path:
  - On RoundTrip error (e.g. connection refused, DNS NXDOMAIN, TLS handshake failure), the sink
    receives an APICallLog with status_code=0, error_message=err.Error(), duration_ms set to the
    measured elapsed time, response_body nil, and the same redacted endpoint as the success path.

